From 9ac820b6a39d06ed024f5a2da2ae915ab8983b0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 01:44:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] editing: caret navigation, click placement and caret painting
 in editing hosts

Contenteditable text could be typed into but the caret could not be moved,
clicking placed it at the start of the host, and it was never painted.

Layout now records, for inline formatting contexts with editable text, which
DOM text node (or <br>) each laid out character came from and its UTF-16
offset, accounting for white space collapsing. Text fragments of such content
carry their character ranges, as text controls already did, and a new caret
stops query returns the laid out caret positions of an editing host by line,
as DOM boundary points with viewport x positions. Lines ended by a <br> that
would hold no text get an empty fragment so the caret has a place there.

Script sends the selection of the focused editing host with each reflow and
layout paints its caret (blinking like text controls) or selected text;
[contenteditable] gets the text control selection colour. Selection changes
schedule a reflow.

Selection.modify() is implemented for editable content (character, word,
line, lineboundary and documentboundary; words move like Chrome on Linux) and
drives ArrowLeft/Right/Up/Down, Ctrl+ArrowLeft/Right, Home/End and
Ctrl+Home/End, with Shift extending the selection. A space typed into an
editing host no longer also scrolls the page. Pressing the primary button in
an editing host puts the caret at the nearest caret position, at the end of
the line when pressed past it, or extends the selection with Shift.

Changing contenteditable rebuilds the element's boxes, since layout only
records the DOM origin of text that was editable when its boxes were built.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
